    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62383,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 96,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The median patient age is 22&nbsp;years (range: 6&ndash;70&nbsp;years)&nbsp;&ndash; typically younger than seen with uterine tumours. Patients frequently present with a pelvic mass. Abdominal pain, ascites, and vaginal bleeding are less common manifestations. Rarely, patients present with extraovarian disease {8393302; 9808135; 22311546; 28296680; 30085939; 30074494}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:55:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62384,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 96,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These are rare tumours.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:55:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62385,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 96,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:55:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62386,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 96,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The frequent association of central-type primitive neuroectodermal tumour and glioma with teratoma suggests a germ cell derivation {8393302; 28296680}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:55:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62387,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 96,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Tumours are typically large and solid or solid and cystic {28296680; 8393302}. The solid component is white, tan, or pink. Haemorrhage and necrosis can be prominent. Teratomatous elements, such as waxy sebaceous or gelatinous material, hair, bone, or teeth may be present {28296680; 8393302; 7114357}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:55:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62388,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 96,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "<p>These tumours are characterized either by a small round cell proliferation or by a variable degree of neuronal or glial differentiation. Differentiated central-type tumours may look like ependymoma, astrocytoma, oligodendroglioma, or neurocytoma {19483626; 9100074; 30074494; 6204919; 29107097}. Less-differentiated tumours have an appearance reminiscent of medulloblastoma, ependymoblastoma, medulloepithelioma, or glioblastoma. A component of mature teratoma may be present. In general, tumours show membranous CD99 and nuclear FLI1 expression, and they are often positive for CD56, NSE, and synaptophysin. They are rarely positive for broad-spectrum cytokeratin, and they are negative for desmin {28296680; 30074494}. About 50% of tumours (most commonly differentiated ones) express GFAP {28296680}. Ependymomas may be positive for ER and PR {30074494}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-23T15:57:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62389,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 96,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:55:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
